<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#world-heritage-site">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">World Heritage Site</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as World Heritage Sites</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A World Heritage Site is a landmark or area given legal protection under an international treaty administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> for its cultural, historical or scientific significance. The programme began with the Convention Concerning the Protection of the World Cultural and Natural Heritage, adopted by UNESCO's General Conference on 16 November 1972 and since ratified by 196 states. It grew from the campaign launched in 1960 to save the monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> from the reservoir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>, which moved temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>. Sites are nominated by their host country and chosen by the World Heritage Committee of 21 states parties.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mỹ Sơn and Hội An are <b>World Heritage Sites</b>, and the Bacam are among the few surviving non-Indic indigenous Hindu peoples in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kailasa temple at Ellora, together with the Jain caves of the same site, is among the most ambitious works of rock-cut architecture in the world and part of the Ellora Caves <b>World Heritage Site</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Citadelle Laferrière, the largest fortress in the Americas, and the Sans-Souci Palace were inscribed as a <b>World Heritage Site</b> in 1982.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>More than 80,000 international students and about 200,000 medical tourists a year come to it, its hawker stalls were the first street food to win Michelin stars, the Botanic Gardens are a <b>World Heritage Site</b>, and since 2008 it has staged Formula One's first night race.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tikal National Park was created in 1955, and UNESCO listed it as a <b>World Heritage Site</b> in 1979.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Shrine of Bahá’u’lláh at Bahjí, towards which Bahá’ís turn in prayer, and the Shrine of the Báb on Mount Carmel, surrounded by terraced gardens, receive pilgrims from around the world and were inscribed as a <b>World Heritage Site</b> in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
